This file describes some hints for using tfem with Fedora Linux.
It might also apply to similar systems like RedHat and CentOS.
---------------------------------------------------------------

- Install Fedora and create a user that has administrator rights.

- Install additional packages (using openblas-threads libary):

sudo dnf -y install dkms kernel-devel gcc
sudo dnf -y install gcc-gfortran ctags xfig evince paraview geany \
    gedit vim-enhanced vim-X11 midori gnuplot git-all openblas-threads
sudo dnf -y install texlive 'tex(lineno.sty)' \
    'tex(multirow.sty)' 'tex(appendix.sty)' 'tex(moreverb.sty)'
sudo dnf -y remove bash-completion

- Download gmsh from http://gmsh.info and unpack the .tgz file in your HOME
  directory.

- Add the following to the $HOME/.bash_profile file (adapt: <version> of gmsh).

# limit

ulimit -c 0          # coredumpsize
ulimit -s unlimited  # stack size

# path

gm=gmsh-<version>-Linux
tf=$HOME/tfem/bin

export PATH=$HOME/$gm/bin:$HOME/$tf/bin:$PATH

# modules to build for tfem

export TFEMADDONS="viscoelastic bcf diffuse_interface elastic inertia\
 generalized_stokes viscoelastic_fh surface_advection grid_deformation\
 compressible_fluid projection structures interface_tracking\
 convection_diffusion_supg dfm surface_convection_diffusion linear_elastic"

# set delay in Makefile for tfem

export SLEEP=0

# viewfig viewer

export PSVIEWER=evince
export PDFVIEWER=evince

- Install tfem (see also INSTALL.linux) in $HOME/tfem

- Install MKL or use OpenBLAS for optimized BLAS library. MKL is usually a
  little bit faster on Intel processors and also provides the pardiso parallel
  solver. OpenBLAS is available on Fedora as a package.

- See README.oneAPI for obtaining MKL (and also Intel Fortran).

- See file README.MKL for instructions on using MKL.

- OpenBLAS is available as a shared library in Fedora. To use this
  library do (for the threaded version openblas-threads):

    ln -s /usr/lib64/libopenblasp.so.0 $HOME/tfem/lib/libopenblasp.so

  set

     LIBBLAS = -lopenblasp

  Note, that in Fedora different versions of the library exist:

    /usr/lib64/libopenblas.so.0   for sequential
    /usr/lib64/libopenblasp.so.0  for threads
    /usr/lib64/libopenblaso.so.0  for openmp

  In addition the libraries can have 64 or 64_ appended to the name to denote
  64-bit integers and 64-bit integers with a symbol name suffix. This requires
  an additional install, for example

    sudo dnf install openblas-threads64

  and add the suffix 64 to the library names.

- Metis5 is available as a shared library in Fedora:

    sudo dnf install metis

  To use this library do

    ln -s /usr/lib64/libmetis.so.0 $HOME/tfem/lib/libmetis.so

  and set

     LIBMETIS = -lmetis

  in the Mdefs.mk file.

  In addition the libraries can have 64 or 64_ appended to the name to denote
  64-bit integers and 64-bit integers with a symbol name suffix. This requires
  an additional install, for example

    sudo dnf install metis64

  and add the suffix 64 to the library names.

- UMFPACK is available as a shared library in Fedora:

    sudo dnf install suitesparse

  To use this library do

    ln -s /usr/lib64/libumfpack.so.5 $HOME/tfem/lib/libumfpack.so

  and set

     LIBUMFPACK = -lumfpack

  in the Mdefs.mk file.

  In addition the libraries can have 64 or 64_ appended to the name to denote
  64-bit integers and 64-bit integers with a symbol name suffix. This requires
  an additional install, for example

    sudo dnf install suitesparce64

  and add the suffix 64 to the library names.

- Set a proper name and email for using git:

    git config --global user.name "John Doe"
    git config --global user.email johndoe@example.com

- To get proper 'git log' output with colors, set the environment variable:

    export LESS="MR"   # set prompt style (M) and use color in less

- To get the proper vi editor for committing, set the environment variable:

    export GIT_EDITOR='vim -c "set wrapmargin=8"'

- To get rid of annoying pop-up windows for typeing your username/password when
  using git:

    git config --global core.askPass ""

- To set your username for gitlab once and for all:

    git config --global credential.https://gitlab.tue.nl.username <username>

  where <username> is your username for the gitlab server.
